content.text:
The desktop intro looks striking. The title is large and elegant. The island behind is dimmed. Good.

But note the title "AETHERIA" appears as "AETHER" with the last letters maybe clipped? It shows "AETHER" and then a faint "IA" to the right? Actually the title is "AETHERIA" but the gradient text at the right edge fades to pale pink; the "IA" is there but low contrast against the bright sky. The title spans 410-1030; it looks like "AETHER" with "IA" washed out. The gradient from white to #ffc9da on a pink sky makes the right side low contrast. Maybe darken the intro overlay more behind the title or change the gradient end to a brighter cyan/pink. The right side "IA" is visible faintly. Could improve by adding a stronger text shadow / drop shadow, or darkening the overlay center more. The overlay center is 0.58 dark; the title's right side over the bright sun/sky. Add a `filter: drop-shadow(0 10px 40px rgba(0,0,0,0.55))` to h1? The drop-shadow currently is blue glow. Change to a dark shadow plus glow:
```
filter: drop-shadow(0 10px 34px rgba(0,0,0,0.45)) drop-shadow(0 0 30px rgba(127,209,255,0.18));
```
Or use a subtle background behind the title. Better: adjust the intro radial gradient to be darker at the title area. It's already 0.58. Could increase to 0.68 and widen. But that dims the scene more. It's okay for the intro.

Maybe the title is intentionally gradient and the "IA" is visible. Looking closely, "AETHERIA" letters: A E T H E R I A. I see "A E T H E R" and then the last "IA" in pale pink blending. It's a bit weak. Change the gradient end from #ffc9da to #ff9ec7 (more saturated pink) and add a dark drop shadow. Let's do:
```
background: linear-gradient(115deg,#ffffff 8%,#d8f3ff 32%,#9fd4ff 55%,#ff9ec7 92%);
filter: drop-shadow(0 8px 30px rgba(0,0,0,0.35)) drop-shadow(0 0 38px rgba(127,209,255,0.2));
```
The pink is more saturated, so it stands out. Good.

Also the lede is small and readable. Good.

Now, the intro island is dimmed by the overlay, but the scene is visible. Nice.

Next, verify the Play button click via the capture flow. The default capture auto-clicks a button named "Play". We need to ensure it clicks the intro Play button and not something else. The snapshot shows "Play" button. The capture's selector `button, input[type=button], ...` finds all; filters labels regex. Only one "Play". Good. It clicks with `trial: true` then `click`. The intro Play is visible/enabled. Good. After click, it waits 1s and screenshots. The intro fade takes 0.85s; by 1s it's nearly gone. The UI fades in over 1.1s with 0.1s delay; at 1s it's ~80% opacity. Fine. The screenshot should show the scene with UI.

However, the capture script runs `page.waitForFunction(() => document.fonts.status === 'loaded')` and then `startForCapture`; it may click before the WebGL scene is ready? The module is loaded by then (fonts load after CSS; three.js module fetch may take ~1s on first load). The Play button is static HTML and visible immediately; the capture could click before `__aetheria.start` is defined? Wait, the Play button's click handler is added by main.js. If main.js hasn't loaded/executed yet, clicking Play does nothing, and the intro remains. The capture waits only for fonts loaded and body visible, which could happen before the module executes. This is a risk. In our local test, fonts loaded after module? The module imports 1.4MB three; fonts 80KB. Both network local; three module may take longer. The capture click might happen too early.

To mitigate, the Play button should start the experience even if main.js hasn't loaded? We can add an inline script in index.html that adds a click handler to Play immediately, hiding the intro and setting a flag; main.js can then start with the intro hidden. Or simpler: make the Play button a link? The capture clicks it; if main.js not loaded, nothing happens. We need robust startup.

Option 1: Add an inline script before the module:
```html
<script>
  document.getElementById('play').addEventListener('click', () => {
    document.body.classList.add('started');
    document.getElementById('intro').classList.add('hidden');
    window.__aetheriaStartRequested = true;
  });
</script>
```
Then main.js's startExperience can check `window.__aetheriaStartRequested` and call startExperience after init, or the inline handler and main handler both fire (two listeners). The inline handler hides the intro immediately; main's handler sets `started` and autoRotate. If main isn't loaded when clicked, the intro hides but `started` remains false until main initializes. Main should check the flag at init:
```js
if (window.__aetheriaStartRequested) startExperience()
```
But `startExperience` is defined after createWorld, which is synchronous after imports; by then the flag is set. Good. However, if the user clicks Play before main loads, the intro hides and the scene is blank until main loads; it will appear shortly. Fine.

But if both listeners fire after main loaded, `startExperience` is called twice; guarded by `if (started) return`. The inline handler also runs first? Listeners fire in registration order: inline script registered first, main second. Inline hides intro and sets flag; main's startExperience sets started. Good. If main not loaded, only inline; main checks flag.

Add this inline script. It also handles the intro background click? Not needed.

Also, the capture's auto-start may find multiple buttons? The intro has only Play. The UI theme buttons and Reset view are also buttons, but their labels don't match. Good. But after clicking Play, the capture waits 1s. Fine.

Option 2: Set `CAPTURE_START_SELECTOR` not possible. Inline is best.

Add:
```html
<script>
  (function () {
    var play = document.getElementById('play');
    play.addEventListener('click', function () {
      window.__aetheriaStartRequested = true;
      document.body.classList.add('started');
      var intro = document.getElementById('intro');
      intro.classList.add('hidden');
      intro.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
    });
  })();
</script>
```
This runs after the intro DOM, before the module script. The module script is after the importmap. Good.

In main.js, after defining startExperience (and before adding listeners or after), check:
```js
if (window.__aetheriaStartRequested) startExperience()
```
But startExperience sets `document.body.classList.add('started')`, `intro.classList.add('hidden')`, and schedules; fine.

Also, if the user clicks Play before main loads, the inline handler hides the intro but `controls.autoRotate` remains false until main's check. The check should be after createWorld and controls exist. Add right after the `startExperience` function definition and listeners? It can be just before `animate()`; but startExperience uses controls and world (created). Add after the Play listener setup.

Let's do.

Also, the intro's inline handler sets `aria-hidden`, and main's startExperience also sets it after 900ms; harmless.

Next, test the actual capture script? We can run `CAPTURE_URL=http://localhost:3002 CAPTURE_DIR=screenshots/capture bash capture.sh` to see the final screenshots. But the capture script uses playwright with its own config and may use a different browser. We can test after fixes.

Before that, fix the title gradient. And maybe improve the day/dawn palettes.

Also, check `start.sh` default start: it serves static files and injects live preview. Fine. It detects `index.html` and serves. The `src` and `vendor` files are served. The mime map includes .js, .css, .woff2? The mime map in default-start.mjs:
```
'.html','.js','.css','.json','.svg','.png','.jpg','.webp','.wasm','.glb'
```
No `.woff2`! It will serve `.woff2` as `application/octet-stream`. Browsers may still load fonts with the correct `format('woff2')` even if MIME is octet-stream? Font loading may be blocked by strict MIME? Usually woff2 served as application/octet-stream works in Chrome? It may warn but load. Let's check the network/console: no font errors, and the font rendered (Sora/Inter look correct). The screenshot shows the custom font. So it's fine. But for the published build, the runtime may use a different server; likely fine. Could inline the fonts as base64 to avoid MIME issues, but not necessary.

Also, `vendor/three/three.module.js` is 1.4MB; fine.

Now, the UI uses `backdrop-filter`; in SwiftShader/Chromium it may be expensive but works.

The `default-capture.mjs` uses `CAPTURE_AUTO_START` and clicks Play. It then waits 1s. The intro fade 0.85s; at 1s the intro is opacity ~0.03; fine. The UI fade 1.1s starts after 0.1s, at 1s opacity ~0.8. The screenshot may show the UI slightly faded. To be safe, reduce the UI fade to 0.7s and intro fade to 0.6s. The capture waits 1s, so both fully visible. Let's set `.ui` transition 0.7s ease 0.05s and `.intro` 0.6s. The intro hidden at 0.6s. Good. Also the `aria-hidden` timeout 900ms irrelevant.

Also, the capture may take the screenshot before the theme transition? The default is dusk; no transition at load. Good.

Now, the mobile capture uses 390x844 and clicks Play; after 1s, the UI fully visible. Good.

Let's apply inline script, title gradient, transition durations.

